{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Braiding.MarkovUniqueness","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Braiding.MarkovUniqueness","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Defs"],"declarations":7,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Braiding.MarkovUniqueness.contact_eq_markov_of_isMarkov","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Braiding.MarkovUniqueness.contact_eq_markov_of_isMarkov","kind":"theorem","line":152,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Braiding/MarkovUniqueness.lean#L152-L171","doc":"**What `contact_is_markov` reduces to.**\n\nStated as a theorem rather than prose so the reduction is machine-checked:\nif the contact coefficient is *itself* a Markov functional agreeing with the\nMarkov trace at level `n`, then on the spanned level it simply **is** the\nMarkov trace — no separate identification is needed.\n\nThis is the payoff of the file. `JonesMobiusDiagonal.contact_is_markov`\nassumes an equation; what actually has to be checked is that the diagonal\nresidue satisfies cyclicity and the Markov relation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Braiding.MarkovUniqueness.markov_unique_of_spanning","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Braiding.MarkovUniqueness.markov_unique_of_spanning","kind":"theorem","line":125,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Braiding/MarkovUniqueness.lean#L125-L150","doc":"**Markov-trace uniqueness, given spanning.**\n\nIf every element of the next level is either at level `n` or stabilised from\nlevel `n`, then two Markov functionals agreeing on level `n` agree there\ntoo — so the Markov trace is determined by its defining properties.\n\n🛑 `hspan` is the classical spanning fact `H_{n+1} = H_n + H_n · σ_n · H_n`\n(Jones 1985, Wenzl 1988) and is an **explicit hypothesis**: this corpus does\nnot prove it, and `markov-trace-inductive-limit.lean` records its closure as\nopen. Do not cite this theorem as though the spanning fact were established.\n\nThe `∨` form is the single-term case; the general statement needs additivity\nof `tr` across the spanning decomposition, which is why this is stated for a\ndecomposition that has already been taken. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Braiding.MarkovUniqueness.agree_on_stabilised","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Braiding.MarkovUniqueness.agree_on_stabilised","kind":"theorem","line":97,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Braiding/MarkovUniqueness.lean#L97-L123","doc":"**The inductive step of Markov-trace uniqueness.**\n\nTwo functionals that are cyclic, share the same Markov relation, and already\nagree on level `n` agree on every *stabilised* element `a * σ * b` with\n`a, b` at level `n`.\n\nThis is the whole content of the induction: cyclicity moves `b` to the front\nso that `b * a` is again at level `n`, the Markov relation then peels `σ`\noff, and the level-`n` agreement finishes it. Nothing here is specific to\nHecke algebras — it holds for any ring and any level predicate closed under\nthe product. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Braiding.MarkovUniqueness.IsMarkov.markov","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Braiding.MarkovUniqueness.IsMarkov.markov","kind":"theorem","line":95,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Braiding/MarkovUniqueness.lean#L95-L95","doc":"The Markov relation: stabilising an element of `H_n` multiplies its trace\nby `z`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Braiding.MarkovUniqueness.IsMarkov.tr_comm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Braiding.MarkovUniqueness.IsMarkov.tr_comm","kind":"theorem","line":92,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Braiding/MarkovUniqueness.lean#L92-L92","doc":"Cyclicity: `tr (a * b) = tr (b * a)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Braiding.MarkovUniqueness.IsMarkov.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Braiding.MarkovUniqueness.IsMarkov.mk","kind":"ctor","line":90,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Braiding/MarkovUniqueness.lean#L90-L90","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Braiding.MarkovUniqueness.IsMarkov","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Braiding.MarkovUniqueness.IsMarkov","kind":"structure","line":83,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Braiding/MarkovUniqueness.lean#L83-L95","doc":"**Markov-functional data**, relative to a level predicate `inH` (read: \"lies\nin `H_n`\"), a stabiliser `σ` (read: `σ_n`), and the Markov parameter `z`.\n\nOnly the two properties the uniqueness argument actually consumes are\nrecorded. Normalisation `tr 1 = 1` is deliberately *not* a field: it fixes\nthe base case of the induction, not the step, and including it here would\nsuggest the step needs it. ","signatureChunk":0}]}